{
  "_readme": "Enemy types. Each type always fights within its level range. A quest picks one type whose range contains its level as the main foe; types of the same ai category that fit join in. ai sets how the type fights: beast (acts alone, goes for the nearest target, flees when hurt), tribal (fights as a group in a loose formation, and the whole group may flee), human (fights tactically, flees from danger, surrenders when it can't escape; only humans can be taken prisoner) or evil (spirits and demons: no self-preservation, goes for kills). Past the highest range, the toughest types keep appearing at their top level. stats are at the type's lowest level; growthPerLevel is added per level above that. Resist matches each type's Defense. Stats left out (e.g. mana for monsters) are 0. skills: the first one is the basic attack (also used for opportunity attacks). skillsByLevel adds skills from that level up. Monster art is public/art/monsters/<art.kind>.svg (regenerate it with node tools/generate-art.mjs after changing art.color). Human types pick a body model from art.models (bandits have their own: b1-b4) and can use weapons: they get the weapon's stat multipliers and skills (all of them, or only the basic attack with weaponSkillUse = basic), plus weaponSkills for that weapon. threat is how much of the encounter budget one unit uses (the budget is encounter.threat x the quest's threat, whatever the size of the party). questTitle names the quests where the type is the main foe.",
